────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

I've kept the #682 promise. Running yoyo todo add "buy milk" from the shell no longer prints a green ✓ and then forgets the item. It now refuses, points you to /todo, and names #679.

Before the fix (debug build at 90e69801, in a throwaway repo), the bug was still there:
• `yoyo todo add "buy milk" printed ✓ Added task #1: buy milk` and exited 0.
• `yoyo todo list then printed No tasks` and exited 0.
• An earlier fix had added a stderr note saying the list is per-process, but the success mark still printed and nothing mentioned #679.

After the fix:
• **Refused verbs:** `add`, `done`, `wip` and `remove` (which also change the list), plus `clear`. From the shell these print a refusal on stderr and exit 1. The message says the list only lives inside a running session, shows the matching /todo <verb> … command, and says persistence is tracked in #679. I checked add and clear by hand; the refusal text is the same for all five.
• **Unchanged:** `yoyo todo list`, bare `yoyo todo` and `yoyo todo board` behave exactly as before and exit 0. The `/todo` command inside the interactive session is untouched.
• **Where the code lives:** the refusal comes from one function, `shell_todo_refusal` in `src/commands_todo.rs`, and uses plain ASCII only. The old note helper moved from `src/dispatch_sub.rs` to sit next to it. That file is on the list of oversized modules that aren't allowed to grow, and it dropped from 2166 to 2150 lines.
• **Help text:** yoyo --help no longer lists yoyo todo add ... as a shell command. docs/ never mentioned the shell form, so it needed no change.

Tests added:
• an exact-text check of the refusal
• a check that every refused verb names #679 and /todo <verb>, with no non-ASCII characters
• a check that read-only verbs don't refuse
• a check that `/todo add` inside the session still adds
• a test that runs the real binary and confirms exit 1, no "Added task", and #679 on stderr

I also broke the verb check on purpose once: the exact-text test failed, and it passed again after I restored the file.

